Got it! Here's a clean and simple example that shows **how to use JavaScript to perform an action when a button is clicked** using the `onclick` event handler.

---

### ✅ **Goal**: When the user clicks the button, a message appears or something changes.

---

### 📁 Folder Structure

```
project/
├── index.html
├── style.css
└── script.js
```

---

### 🧾 `index.html`

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8" />
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
  <title>Click Event Example</title>
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css" />
</head>
<body>

  <h1>JavaScript Onclick Example</h1>
  <button id="myButton">Click Me</button>
  <p id="outputText">Waiting for click...</p>

  <script src="script.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

---

### 🎨 `style.css`

```css
body {
  font-family: sans-serif;
  text-align: center;
  margin-top: 100px;
}

button {
  padding: 10px 20px;
  font-size: 16px;
  background-color: #007bff;
  color: white;
  border: none;
  cursor: pointer;
}

button:hover {
  background-color: #0056b3;
}

#outputText {
  margin-top: 20px;
  font-size: 18px;
  color: #333;
}
```

---

### ⚙️ `script.js`

```javascript
// Get the button and paragraph element
const button = document.getElementById("myButton");
const output = document.getElementById("outputText");

// Add click event listener to the button
button.onclick = function () {
  output.innerText = "You clicked the button!";
  output.style.color = "green";
};
```

---

### ✅ Result

When the user clicks the "Click Me" button, the text changes from **"Waiting for click..."** to **"You clicked the button!"**, and the color turns green.

