In **HTML and CSS**, to create a **hover effect** using **keyframes**, you generally:

1. Use a `:hover` selector to **trigger animation**.
2. Define the animation using `@keyframes`.
3. Apply animation to an element by **class** or **ID**.

---

### ✅ **1. Using a Class with Hover and Keyframes**

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <style>
    .box {
      width: 100px;
      height: 100px;
      background-color: blue;
      transition: all 0.3s ease-in-out;
    }

    .box:hover {
      animation: bounce 0.5s ease forwards;
    }

    @keyframes bounce {
      0% { transform: translateY(0); }
      50% { transform: translateY(-20px); }
      100% { transform: translateY(0); }
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <div class="box"></div>
</body>
</html>
```

---

### ✅ **2. Using an ID with Hover and Keyframes**

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <style>
    #circle {
      width: 100px;
      height: 100px;
      border-radius: 50%;
      background-color: red;
      transition: all 0.3s ease;
    }

    #circle:hover {
      animation: spin 1s linear infinite;
    }

    @keyframes spin {
      0% { transform: rotate(0deg); }
      100% { transform: rotate(360deg); }
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <div id="circle"></div>
</body>
</html>
```

---

### 🔁 Key Notes:
- Use `.classname:hover` or `#idname:hover` to **apply animation on hover**.
- `@keyframes` defines how the animation progresses.
- `animation:` specifies the name, duration, easing, direction, etc.

